From bcbfae6761016974d9185cecee333b5f7aaf0442 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Will Rose <williamdanrose@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 02:51:23 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] QQuickTextInput: don't call QMimeData::text() for canPaste

QQuickTextInput::canPaste() and q_canPasteChanged() call
QMimeData::text() to reject an advertised text format whose payload is
empty. Calling text() can require synchronous IPC with another
process and block when the clipboard owner is slow or unresponsive.

Use QMimeData::hasText() in both paths, as QQuickTextEdit already does
through format checks in QQuickTextControl. This keeps the existing cache
and canPasteChanged() signal semantics without requesting the advertised
text payload from either the property getter or clipboard-change handler.

This restores the formats-only behavior that preceded 580715fe58 and
6c07e2a762. A provider that advertises text but returns an empty payload
is therefore considered pasteable; an actually empty clipboard remains
unpasteable.

Add a regression test that counts QMimeData::retrieveData() calls and
covers the initial property read, clipboard changes, unchanged
availability, and the advertised-empty-payload case.

[ChangeLog][QtQuick][TextInput] canPaste now reflects whether the
clipboard advertises a text format without calling QMimeData::text() to
inspect the payload. A provider that advertises text but returns an empty
payload is therefore considered pasteable.

